# =============================================================================
# .env.example - the configuration contract for the blog platform.
#
# Fifteen variables span the two tiers, and these fifteen are the whole of the
# configuration the application reads. Twelve belong to the service tier and three to
# the presentation tier; there is no sixteenth, and a key that is not below is not
# read by anything.
#
# Nothing is hard-coded: no secret, no connection string and no origin or host list
# appears anywhere in the source tree. SIX of the twelve backend variables have no
# default in source at all - DATABASE_URL, JWT_SECRET_KEY, CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS,
# ENVIRONMENT, SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL and SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD - so an absent value stops
# the process while it is still starting instead of selecting a connection string, a
# trusted origin list, a deployment stage or an administrator credential that nobody
# chose. Those six are marked REQUIRED below. The six that do carry a default -
# JWT_ALGORITHM, the two token lifetimes, LOG_LEVEL, AUTH_RATE_LIMIT and
# MAX_REQUEST_BODY_BYTES - default to a safe value rather than a convenient one, and
# none of them is a secret, a host or an origin.
#
# How to use it
#   cp .env.example .env                          # backend + docker compose
#   grep '^NEXT_PUBLIC_' .env.example \
#     > frontend/.env.local                        # frontend: the 3 public keys
#
# Copy ONLY the NEXT_PUBLIC_ lines into frontend/.env.local. Next.js loads env
# files from its own project directory, and everything in that file is readable by
# the Next.js build and server process, so copying the whole file would hand the
# signing key, the database credential and the administrator password to a process
# that needs none of them. Least privilege applies to a developer's own machine
# too.
#
# .env and .env.local are git-ignored; this example is the only environment file
# that is ever committed, so every value below is a PLACEHOLDER. They are chosen
# to work against the local docker compose stack out of the box, and every one of
# them must be changed for any non-local use. The three CREDENTIAL placeholders
# are enforced, not merely advised: JWT_SECRET_KEY, SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD and
# SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL are compared against the values below at startup and REJECTED
# unless ENVIRONMENT is development or test.
#
# Who reads what
#   backend   backend/app/core/config.py is the ONLY module that reads the
#             environment (pydantic-settings). Every other module imports the
#             settings object from it, so this file is the single contract. It
#             declares twelve fields and tolerates exactly the three presentation-
#             tier names below, the NEXT_PUBLIC_ values; ANY other key in an env
#             file - a typo such as DATABSE_URL, a stale key from an older
#             revision, a fourth NEXT_PUBLIC_ value - fails startup with a message
#             naming it, rather than being ignored while the field it was meant for
#             keeps a default.
#   frontend  the three NEXT_PUBLIC_ values are inlined into the client bundle at
#             build time and are therefore public. Never give a secret that prefix.
#   compose   docker compose reads the repository-root .env automatically. It
#             declares its own defaults for the PostgreSQL container, so no
#             container-only variable belongs in this contract - see the header
#             of docker-compose.yml for the overrides parallel checkouts need.
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line, no `export`, no spaces around `=`. Values are
# unquoted except where one contains a space (only NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_NAME does),
# which keeps the file loadable by pydantic-settings, by docker compose and by
# `set -a; . ./.env; set +a` alike.
#
# Encoding: UTF-8. The backend reads its env files as utf-8-sig, so a leading
# byte-order mark - which PowerShell's Out-File and some Windows editors write when
# they say "UTF-8" - is discarded rather than becoming part of the first key's name.
# docker compose and `set -a; . ./.env` do NOT strip one, so prefer UTF-8 without a
# BOM if the same file has to serve all three.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BACKEND (11 variables) - read by backend/app/core/config.py
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Database ----------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED, no default. PostgreSQL 18 connection URL, validated at startup as a
# complete DSN: correct scheme, a host, a port inside 1-65535 if one is given, and
# a database name. A libpq query string such as ?sslmode=require is allowed; a
# fragment is not. The scheme MUST be postgresql+psycopg://, because
# psycopg 3 is the single driver in this project: it serves both the async
# application engine and the synchronous engine Alembic drives. Do not use
# postgresql:// (SQLAlchemy resolves that to psycopg2, which is not installed) or
# postgresql+asyncpg:// (asyncpg is deliberately not a dependency).
# The placeholder user, password, port and database match the defaults
# docker-compose.yml gives its `db` service, so `docker compose up -d db` and this
# URL agree without further editing. They are local placeholders, not credentials.
# Run the test suite against a SEPARATE database: the contract is the keys in this
# file, so a test run points DATABASE_URL at another database (blog_test, say)
# rather than introducing a variable of its own.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://blog:blog@localhost:5432/blog

# --- Authentication ----------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED, no default. HMAC key that signs every access and refresh token.
# HARD REQUIREMENT: at least as many BYTES as JWT_ALGORITHM hashes to - 32 for
# HS256, 48 for HS384, 64 for HS512. Startup validation measures the key in BYTES
# against the algorithm actually configured and refuses to boot below that floor,
# because RFC 7518 s3.2 requires an HMAC key at least as large as the hash output
# and that is the same comparison PyJWT makes before raising
# InsecureKeyLengthWarning. A 32-byte key is therefore fine for HS256 and rejected
# under HS384 or HS512. Bytes, not characters: a passphrase of accented or CJK
# characters encodes to more bytes than it has characters.
# The placeholder clears the HS256 floor so a freshly copied file starts, but it is
# published in this repository and therefore worthless as a secret: it is REJECTED
# outright unless ENVIRONMENT is development or test. Generate a unique value per
# environment and never reuse one:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
JWT_SECRET_KEY=change-me-change-me-change-me-change-me

# Token signing algorithm. HS256 is the documented default and is what the single
# symmetric key above supports; an RS or ES algorithm would need a key pair.
# Raising this raises the minimum size of JWT_SECRET_KEY above - HS384 needs 48
# bytes and HS512 needs 64 - so change both together or startup will fail.
JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256

# Access-token lifetime, in minutes. Deliberately short: an access token is a
# bearer credential that cannot be withdrawn before it expires.
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=15

# Refresh-token lifetime, in days. Refresh tokens rotate on every use and are
# stored only as hashes, so this bounds an idle session rather than an active one.
REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRE_DAYS=7

# --- Cross-origin access -----------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED, no default. Browser origins permitted to call the API,
# COMMA-SEPARATED - not JSON, not space-separated. Each entry is a complete
# origin: http or https scheme, host and port, with no userinfo, no path, no query
# and no trailing slash - startup validation rejects every one of those, because
# CORSMiddleware compares the Origin header verbatim and a malformed entry can
# only ever fail silently in someone's browser. `*` is accepted as the whole list,
# alone and deliberately; mixing it with named origins is rejected, because the
# middleware reads it as "allow every origin" and the named entries beside it would
# look like a restriction that is not being applied.
#
# An EMPTY value is rejected, not treated as "none". `CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=` would
# start a service that reports itself healthy, answers every request made by a tool
# and fails every request made by the browser tier it exists to serve - in the
# browser, with a message that names CORS rather than this variable.
#
# Two spellings are CANONICALISED rather than rejected, because both are correct in
# intent and only one of them can ever match: the scheme and host are lower-cased,
# and a port that is the scheme's default is dropped. A browser sends
# `https://example.com`, never `HTTPS://Example.com` and never `https://example.com:443`,
# so those entries would otherwise be dead. Duplicates that collapse to one origin
# are collapsed.
#
# Both loopback spellings are listed because the
# development server advertises localhost while the end-to-end runner drives
# 127.0.0.1, and a browser treats those as different origins.
CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://127.0.0.1:3000

# --- Observability -----------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED, no default: development | test | staging | production.
# There is deliberately no fallback, because every effect of this variable is a
# security decision. It selects console-readable versus JSON structured logging; it
# decides whether the two INTERACTIVE documentation routes (/docs and /redoc) are
# exposed; it decides whether Strict-Transport-Security is pinned; and it decides
# whether the credential placeholders in this file are accepted at all. Defaulting
# it would mean a deployment that forgot to set it ran with the least hardened
# profile of the four.
#
# /openapi.json is NOT gated by this variable. The machine-readable contract is
# served in every stage, deliberately: it is the API's published description, it
# drives no requests by itself, and withdrawing it would leave a client unable to
# discover the surface it is entitled to call. What production withdraws is the two
# human renderings, because an interactive console that can drive real credentials
# against real data has no audience there.
#   development  human-readable logs, /docs and /redoc exposed, placeholders accepted
#   test         JSON logs, /docs and /redoc exposed, placeholders accepted, rate
#                limiter disabled so a suite that exercises login and refresh
#                repeatedly cannot go flaky
#   staging      JSON logs, /docs and /redoc exposed, placeholders REJECTED, HSTS
#                not pinned by default
#   production   JSON logs, /docs and /redoc WITHDRAWN, placeholders REJECTED,
#                HSTS pinned
ENVIRONMENT=development

# Logging threshold: CRITICAL | ERROR | WARNING | INFO | DEBUG.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- Rate limiting -----------------------------------------------------------

# Limit applied to the authentication routes, as a slowapi/limits expression:
# "<count>/<period>", where period is second, minute, hour or day - for example
# 5/minute or 100/hour. Several limits may be combined with a semicolon, as in
# 5/minute;100/hour. Five attempts a minute is generous for a person signing in and
# useless for a script replaying credentials; raise it deliberately if a deployment
# genuinely needs more.
#
# The limit is counted per calling address, taken from the connection itself and never
# from a header the caller sent. There is no variable for that here because it is not
# configurable: a request that carries X-Forwarded-For, X-Real-IP or Forwarded is
# counted against one shared bucket instead, so rotating such a header cannot reset the
# limit, and the access log records no origin for it rather than the one the caller
# nominated. Uvicorn's own --proxy-headers is ON by default and rewrites the address the
# application sees for a loopback peer, which is exactly what that rule neutralises.
#
# OPERATIONAL REQUIREMENT if a reverse proxy or sidecar is ever placed in front of this
# service: run uvicorn/gunicorn with --forwarded-allow-ips=<the proxy's address> (or
# --no-proxy-headers if the proxy does not forward the client at all) rather than leaving
# the default, and revisit app/core/rate_limit.py and app/middleware/request_context.py
# together so the address the service throttles on and the address it logs stay the same
# decision. Do not set FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS=* - it lets any caller nominate its own
# address, which is the state both of those modules exist to prevent.
AUTH_RATE_LIMIT=5/minute

# Largest request body, in bytes, that the API will read. A body whose Content-Length
# declares more is refused with 413 before the application is called; one that declares
# no length, or declares the wrong one, is refused as soon as the bytes actually
# delivered cross the ceiling. app/middleware/body_limit.py enforces both, and it does
# so above the framework - a field bound on a schema and the rate limit on the
# authentication routes are both applied only after the body has been read and parsed,
# so neither is a limit on request SIZE.
#
# The default of 1048576 (1 MiB) is chosen against the largest body any route legitimately
# accepts, not picked round: a post's content is bounded at 100 000 characters and a
# comment at 5 000, and no route in this API takes a file upload - a cover image is a URL
# reference - so there is no multipart body to accommodate. The floor accepted is 65536;
# a value below the largest body a route accepts would make that route answer 413 for
# every valid request, which reads as a broken endpoint rather than as a misconfiguration.
#
# This is the application's own bound and is deliberately not a substitute for one at the
# edge. A production deployment should also bound request size at its reverse proxy, which
# refuses the traffic earlier; this exists because the service must not depend on a proxy
# it cannot see, and because the development and test servers have none in front of them.
MAX_REQUEST_BODY_BYTES=1048576

# --- Seed data ---------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED, no default. Administrator account created by `make seed`
# (python -m app.db.seed). Seeding is idempotent, so re-running it will not
# overwrite an account that already exists: set both values before the first seed
# run rather than after it. The placeholder below uses example.com, which RFC 2606
# reserves and no deployment can receive mail at, and it is REJECTED unless
# ENVIRONMENT is development or test.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com

# REQUIRED, no default. CHANGE THIS - the value below will NOT start the service.
# It is the password of the only ADMIN principal the seed creates, and a committed
# placeholder is public knowledge, so this exact value is rejected in EVERY stage:
# the one account this project creates itself must not be the one credential an
# attacker already knows.
# Whatever you put here is held to exactly the policy POST /api/v1/auth/register
# applies, and checked at startup rather than at seed time: at least 12 characters,
# at most 128, drawn from at least 3 of the 5 character groups (lowercase,
# uppercase, digit, a caseless letter, anything else). The 128 ceiling matters as
# much as the floor - the login route applies the same bound, so a longer value
# would hash and store fine and then be refused at every sign-in.
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=ChangeMe-Admin-Password-1


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FRONTEND (3 variables) - read by Next.js
#
# Copy this block into frontend/.env.local - `grep '^NEXT_PUBLIC_'` does it -
# because Next.js loads env files from its own project directory and there is no
# reason for the frontend process to hold a backend secret. The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix
# inlines a value into the client bundle, so all three are public by design and none
# may ever hold a secret.
#
# ONE base URL serves both execution contexts, and that is a deployment
# responsibility rather than a second variable. NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL is the
# address of the API, and a Server Component, a route handler and a browser all use
# it - so a deployment has to make that one address resolve from inside the
# deployment as well as from a reader's network, which is what a published ingress,
# a shared hostname or a reverse proxy already does. Adding a server-only second
# address would grow the configuration contract to sixteen keys to work around a
# routing decision.
#
# Both tiers share the repository-root file, so the backend settings model tolerates
# these three names - and ONLY these three, by exact spelling. It does not ignore
# unknown keys generally: a fourth value added here without being added to
# _FRONTEND_ENV_KEYS in backend/app/core/config.py will stop the backend from
# starting, which is the same protection that turns a misspelt DATABSE_URL into an
# error instead of a silent default.
#
# The block is CLOSED at three, and the temptation it is closed against is worth
# naming: the remote-image host allow-list looks like configuration and is not. It
# is declared in frontend/src/lib/utils.ts, in source, because next.config.ts is
# evaluated once at build time - so an environment-supplied list would read as
# run-time configuration while behaving as a build-time constant - and because that
# list decides which third parties a reader's browser is asked to contact, which is
# a reviewed code change rather than a deployment setting. See the header of
# frontend/next.config.ts.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of the REST API. It INCLUDES the /api/v1 prefix and carries no trailing
# slash, because frontend/src/lib/api/client.ts appends bare resource paths such as
# /posts to it. Repeating the prefix in client code would request
# /api/v1/api/v1/posts, so the prefix lives here and only here.
#
# It is read in exactly one place - frontend/src/lib/api/client.ts - and by both
# execution contexts: a browser fetching from a client island, and the Next.js server
# process rendering a Server Component, a route handler or the generated sitemap. So
# the value must be an address that resolves from BOTH, which is a deployment
# decision: publish the API at one hostname and route it, rather than configuring a
# second internal address here. A server render that needs a credential passes it per
# request through the transport's `bearer` option; the credential store is
# browser-only, so no per-reader token is ever held in a module global on the server.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1

# Canonical site origin. It must be absolute, must carry a scheme and must NOT end
# in a slash: metadataBase, every canonical link, each sitemap entry and the
# BlogPosting/Person structured data are all built by joining paths onto it, so a
# stray trailing slash corrupts every URL a crawler is given.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Site name used by the title template and the social cards. Quoted because the
# value contains a space; the shell, python-dotenv, Node and docker compose all
# strip the quotes.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_NAME="Modern Blog"
